{"id":"n-20261007-lg5k4w","category":"tech","display_title":"AI Meme Sparks Legal Battle","display_title_he":"מם בינה מלאכותית מעורר קרב משפטי","short_summary":"An internet meme created with artificial intelligence has triggered an international legal dispute over copyright and ownership.","english_level":"C1","words":524,"reading_time":4,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/oct/06/brain-rot-tung-tung-tung-sahur-noxa-openai-video","title":"Who owns ‘brain rot’? Inside the international legal drama over a beloved meme","published_at":"2026-10-06T14:00:16.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-06T14:00:16.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-07T06:12:58.102Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The boundary between human creativity and artificial intelligence is currently being tested in an international legal battle over a viral internet character. The character, known as Tung Tung Tung Sahur or Triple T, emerged online in 2025. According to reports, an Indonesian teenager using the online username Noxa generated the character with OpenAI's image-generation tool, basing the design on a traditional drum used during Ramadan. The stick-like figure quickly evolved into a global phenomenon within the absurdist internet subgenre often referred to as Italian brain rot, beloved by generation Alpha and younger members of gen Z alongside creations like Ballerina Cappuccina and Tralalero Tralala. Across platforms like YouTube and TikTok, videos featuring the character have accumulated hundreds of millions of views, inspiring fan art, animations, cosplay, plushies, perfume, stickers, and a forthcoming appearance in Fortnite.","However, this widespread virality has sparked a complex financial and legal dispute. A lawsuit was filed in a United States district court in California's northern district after video game companies and a French creative agency clashed over commercial rights to use the character in a popular Roblox game named Steal a Brainrot, which was released by Do Big Studios. The French agency, Mementum Lab, co-founded by Eben Jeda, represents the creator and seeks to modernize economic infrastructure for internet-native works. According to agency representatives, digital creations can achieve global reach almost instantly while leaving their originators economically invisible. Conversely, opponents and online creators like Fabian Mosele argue that memes are collectively owned by the online communities that amplify them, suggesting that gatekeeping ownership to the initial prompter disregards the contributions of countless users who brought the characters to life.","The core of the legal disagreement centers on United States copyright law, which traditionally applies exclusively to human-made creations. While a 2025 United States Copyright Office report stated that original expressions created by human authors receive protection even if they incorporate AI-generated material, it simultaneously ruled out purely automated content or material lacking sufficient human control, noting that authorship must be analyzed on a case-by-case basis. Legal representatives for the gaming companies, including lawyer Aaron Moss from Mitchell Silberberg & Knupp LLP, argue that using sparse prompts—specifically typing a few basic instructions into an image generator—does not constitute genuine human authorship. Court documents revealed that the character was developed over fifteen minutes using seven basic prompts, such as drawing a pentungan sahur with a face and legs.","This dispute highlights the complications of enforcing intellectual property rights in a borderless digital landscape. While the French agency successfully utilized the European Union's Digital Services Act to remove the character from the Roblox game, it simultaneously pursued trademark infringement claims in a California court alongside Steven Stein of Greenberg Glusker, despite not yet holding a US trademark. Legal experts like James Grimmelmann of Cornell Tech note that the outcome of this case could establish crucial precedents, concrete decisions, and widespread norms regarding artificial intelligence, intellectual property, and digital creativity for years to come. Meanwhile, content creators like Adam Aleksic observe that the mythological status and viral frenzy surrounding the character reflect how internet memes function as empty vehicles and tools for collective storytelling."],"facts":[{"fact":"An Indonesian teen created the viral internet character Tung Tung Tung Sahur in 2025 using OpenAI's image generator.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"A lawsuit in a US district court in California involves a games company suing the French creative agency Mementum over the right to use the character in a Roblox game.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"United States copyright law requires human authorship, creating a legal battle over whether sparse AI prompting qualifies for protection.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"The","lemma":"the","pos":"DET","he":"ה־","name":false},{"text":"boundary","lemma":"boundary","pos":"NOUN","he":"גבול","name":false},{"text":"between","lemma":"between","pos":"ADP","he":"בין","name":false},{"text":"human","lemma":"human","pos":"ADJ","he":"אנושי","name":false},{"text":"creativity","lemma":"creativity","pos":"NOUN","he":"יצירתיות","name":false},{"text":"and","lemma":"and","pos":"CCONJ","he":"וגם","name":false},{"text":"artificial","lemma":"artificial","pos":"ADJ","he":"מלאכותי","name":false},{"text":"intelligence","lemma":"intelligence","pos":"NOUN","he":"בינה","name":false},{"text":"is","lemma":"be","pos":"AUX","he":"הוא","name":false},{"text":"currently","lemma":"currently","pos":"ADV","he":"כעת","name":false},{"text":"being","lemma":"be","pos":"AUX","he":"נמצא ב־","name":false},{"text":"tested","lemma":"test","pos":"VERB","he":"נבחן","name":false},{"text":"i